John's work is 30 miles away, and he has to go there and back each day. Therefore, the total miles driven per day is:
\[
30 \, \text{miles (to work)} + 30 \, \text{miles (back home)} = 60 \, \text{miles per day.}
\]

### Step 2: Determine the total miles driven per week
John works 5 days a week, so the total miles driven per week is:
\[
60 \, \text{miles per day} \times 5 \, \text{days per week} = 300 \, \text{miles per week.}
\]

### Step 3: Determine the total miles driven per year
John works 50 weeks a year, so the total miles driven per year is:
\[
300 \, \text{miles per week} \times 50 \, \text{weeks per year} = 15,000 \, \text{miles per year.}
\]

### Step 4: Calculate the cost for mileage
John is charged $2 per mile driven. Therefore, the total cost for mileage is:
\[
15,000 \, \text{miles per year} \times 2 \, \text{dollars per mile} = 30,000 \, \text{dollars.}
\]

### Step 5: Calculate the total bonus paid per year
John gives his driver a $150 bonus per month. There are 12 months in a year, so the total bonus paid per year is:
\[
150 \, \text{dollars per month} \times 12 \, \text{months per year} = 1,800 \, \text{dollars.}
\]

### Step 6: Calculate the total cost for the year
The total cost for the year is the sum of the mileage cost and the bonus:
\[
30,000 \, \text{dollars (mileage cost)} + 1,800 \, \text{dollars (bonus)} = 31,800 \, \text{dollars.}
\]

